Overlay patch 0040: run the Satella boot phase only where its consumer
exists (gate on ENABLE_SCRIPTING). Device feedback (2026-07-21): first
launch shows "Connecting to Satella..." and BLOCKS for seconds while
RunExtract's GS_COMPILE runs Satella::Client::Execute() before loading
resources. Satella (satella.net64.dev) is a ONE-SHOT at boot:
Connecting -> FetchingKeys -> Done. Those keys are for MOD SIGNATURE
VERIFICATION, and the relay API they enable is used by SCRIPTED mods — both
require ENABLE_SCRIPTING, which is compiled OUT on iOS (overlay 0004/0006).
So on iOS the phase connects, fetches keys nothing reads (0006 rev2 makes
the iOS keystore registration a no-op), and just walls the user behind the
modal — pure delay, no function. This is NOT a platform disable: gate the
phase on `defined(ENABLE_SCRIPTING)` so Satella runs on desktop (and on iOS
too if scripting is ever enabled) and is skipped only where it has nothing
to do. Respects upstream intent + auto-restores with scripting. Answers the
Q-003 note ("flip it if device testing shows hangs") without removing the
feature. Asset/o2r texture packs never used Satella (AddArchive has no
signature gate), so pack loading is unaffected.

--- a/src/port/Engine.cpp
+++ b/src/port/Engine.cpp
@@ -904,7 +904,13 @@
                 continue;
             }
             case GS_COMPILE: {
-#ifdef USE_NETWORKING
+                // GS_IOS (overlay 0040): Satella fetches mod-signing keys +
+                // enables the scripted-mod relay — both need ENABLE_SCRIPTING.
+                // When scripting is compiled out (iOS) the phase has no
+                // consumer and only adds a blocking "Connecting..." delay, so
+                // gate it on scripting rather than platform (auto-restores if
+                // scripting is ever enabled on iOS).
+#if defined(USE_NETWORKING) && defined(ENABLE_SCRIPTING)
                 if (CVarGetInteger(CVAR_DEVELOPER_TOOLS("Satella"), 1) == 1) {
                     threadPool->submit_task([&]() -> void {
                         Satella::Client::Instance().Execute();
